1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,020
A magazine paid millions for fake diaries with the wrong initials.

2
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:06,300
West Germany, the early nineteen eighties.

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:11,520
A man named Konrad Kujau sells old war souvenirs in Stuttgart.

4
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:16,280
Then he starts hand-writing notebooks, and claims they're Hitler's secret diaries.

5
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:19,160
He sells them under a fake name, through a middleman.

6
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:23,520
Then a star reporter at Stern, a big German magazine, gets them.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:27,380
His name is Gerd Heidemann, and he actually believes they're real.

8
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:29,960
Hit like if you can already smell the fake.

9
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:33,520
He talks his bosses into paying about nine million marks.

10
00:00:34,160 --> 00:00:37,340
Marks were West Germany's money back then, and this was a fortune.

11
00:00:37,860 --> 00:00:40,980
And Heidemann? He secretly kept a big chunk for himself.

12
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:45,000
In April nineteen eighty-three, the magazine tells the whole world.

13
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:48,580
But nobody really checked the most obvious thing, the cover.

14
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:52,540
On the covers were plastic letters from a shop, F and H.

15
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:57,780
But Hitler's first name, Adolf, starts with an A. It should have said A H.

16
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:00,800
Send this to the friend who never proofreads anything.

17
00:01:01,640 --> 00:01:04,700
Then Germany's national archives tested the paper and the ink.

18
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,380
Both were modern, way too new to be real.

19
00:01:07,740 --> 00:01:10,000
And that took less than two weeks after the announcement.

20
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:14,220
They called it a crude forgery, which means a cheap, obvious fake.

21
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:18,520
Follow if you love when one tiny detail sinks a giant lie.

22
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,860
In nineteen eighty-five, a court in Hamburg convicted Kujau and Heidemann.

23
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:25,960
Each was sentenced to about four and a half years in prison.

24
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:29,840
Millions of marks, and nobody checked two letters on the cover.

25
00:01:30,360 --> 00:01:32,160
Would you have caught it? Yes or no.

